From: Connor O'Brien <connor.obrien@redwoodinference.com>
To: Jordan Lee <jordan.lee@acme-retail.com>, Priya Shah <priya.shah@acme-retail.com>, Marissa Cole <marissa.cole@redwoodinference.com>
Cc: Rishi Malhotra <rishi.malhotra@redwoodinference.com>, Dan Miller <dan.miller@acme-retail.com>
Date: 2026-03-15T13:00:00-07:00
Subject: Re: Acme POC  Integration plan, success criteria, and next steps

Hi team  quick technical summary and concrete integration steps (happy to walk through live at kickoff):

1) Auth & provisioning
- We issue an API key scoped to the POC workspace. For additional network isolation we can add IP allowlist entries and a VPC endpoint (requires SRE config).

2) SDK / API routes (examples)
- Non-streaming: POST https://api.redwoodinference.com/v1/generate
  payload: {"model":"redwood-2-large","input":"...","max_tokens":256}
- Streaming (recommended for chat): websocket or SSE to https://api.redwoodinference.com/v1/stream with same payload shape; we support both SSE and WebSocket.
- Function/tool calling: include a "tools" schema in the payload. Example: {"tool_calling":true, "tools":[{"name":"inventory_lookup","schema":{...}}]}
  when model returns a tool call, the SDK exposes structured metadata (tool_name + args) and a call_id to correlate.

3) SDK snippets (attached: redwood-sdk-snippets.md)
- Node example (JS):
  const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RDW_KEY });
  // normal generate
  const res = await client.generate({ model: "redwood-2-large", input: prompt });
  // streaming (WebSocket)
  const ws = client.stream({ model: "redwood-2-large", input: prompt });
  ws.on('chunk', chunk => { /* update UI */ });
  // function calling
  client.on('tool_call', async ({ tool_name, args, call_id }) => {
    // call your backend, then client.toolResponse(call_id, { result: ... });
  });

4) Performance tuning & batching recommendations
- For 256-token median requests target: set batching window to 10 25ms and batch size cap at 16 to preserve latency.
- Enable prefix/KV caching for repeated session prompts (saves tokens).
- Quantization profile: use int8 for cost/latency balance during POC; we can test int4 if quality is equal.

5) Observability & limits
- We will provide per-route latency + token usage dashboards. Error codes: 429 (rate), 503 (capacity). We'll set a POC soft quota and alerting.

I'll attach the SDK snippets and a one-page streaming example to the thread (redwood-sdk-snippets.md). If you need a runnable example repo I can share a temporary link before kickoff.

Thanks  looking forward to the kickoff.
Connor O'Brien
Solutions Engineer, Redwood Inference
connor.obrien@redwoodinference.com
